Bootstrap: docker
From: nvidia/cuda:12.4.1-cudnn-runtime-ubuntu22.04

# One image for every target platform. The build is pinned so that a `.sif` built today and one
# built next year contain the same thing -- which is the entire point of shipping a container rather
# than an environment file.
#
#   apptainer build oceanml3d.sif container/oceanml3d.def
#   apptainer exec --nv oceanml3d.sif oceanml3d command=list-models
#
# WHY cu124 ON A 12.1 DRIVER
# CUDA minor-version compatibility: a build against any CUDA 12.x runs on any driver that supports
# 12.0 or later (>= 525). Datarmor reports driver 530.30.02 / CUDA 12.1, so cu124 is fine there, and
# it is equally fine on newer drivers elsewhere. The alternative, cu118, would work too but gives up
# performance on Ampere and Hopper; there is no cu121 wheel for torch 2.6.
#
# WHY torch 2.6
# `monai>=1.5,<1.6` -- a hard dependency since MONAI's DiffusionModelUNet became the default gridded
# trunk -- pins torch>=2.4.1,<2.7. 2.6.0 is the top of that window. Raising it means raising the
# monai ceiling first, and monai 1.6 wants torch>=2.8.
#
# ARCHITECTURES COVERED by the cu124 wheels, i.e. the GPUs this image is expected to meet:
#   sm_70  Tesla V100          Datarmor, Jean Zay (V100 partition)
#   sm_75  Quadro RTX 8000     Odyssey
#   sm_80  A100                Jean Zay
#   sm_90  H100                Jean Zay (H100 partition)
# Note for V100: fp16 mixed precision works, bf16 does not. Use `training.trainer.precision=16-mixed`
# there, never `bf16-mixed`.

%files
    pyproject.toml /opt/oceanml3d/pyproject.toml
    README.md      /opt/oceanml3d/README.md
    oceanml3d      /opt/oceanml3d/oceanml3d

%post
    set -eux
    export DEBIAN_FRONTEND=noninteractive
    apt-get update
    apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends \
        python3.10 python3.10-venv python3-pip git ca-certificates libgl1 libglib2.0-0
    rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*
    update-alternatives --install /usr/bin/python3 python3 /usr/bin/python3.10 1
    ln -sf /usr/bin/python3 /usr/local/bin/python

    python3 -m pip install --no-cache-dir -U pip setuptools wheel

    # torch first, from the CUDA index, so the package install below cannot pull a different build.
    python3 -m pip install --no-cache-dir \
        torch==2.6.0 --index-url https://download.pytorch.org/whl/cu124

    # The package itself, so its metadata, console script and entry points are what gets exercised.
    # `prepare`: the downloads (copernicusmarine, argopy, cdsapi). Datarmor never needs them -- it
    # mirrors GLORYS and the Argo GDAC -- but Jean Zay has nothing else, and the runbook there
    # said copernicusmarine was in the image when it was not.
    python3 -m pip install --no-cache-dir -e '/opt/oceanml3d[dev,zarr,logging,prepare]'

    python3 - <<'PY'
import torch
assert torch.__version__.startswith("2.6"), torch.__version__
assert "+cu124" in torch.__version__, torch.__version__
import monai, xarray, netCDF4, dask, einops, scipy, tensorboard, copernicusmarine, argopy  # noqa: F401
print("built:", torch.__version__, "| monai", monai.__version__)
PY

%environment
    export PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1
    export PYTHONUNBUFFERED=1
    # Leave threading to the job script: every centre sizes cores differently, and the default of
    # "one thread per core on the node" is wrong on a shared node.
    export OMP_NUM_THREADS=${OMP_NUM_THREADS:-1}

%runscript
    exec oceanml3d "$@"

%labels
    org.opencontainers.image.title oceanml3d-core
    org.opencontainers.image.licenses EUPL-1.2
    cuda 12.4
    torch 2.6.0+cu124

%help
    oceanml3d-core: 4DVarNet and flow matching for 3D multivariate ocean data assimilation.

    Run from a clone. /opt/oceanml3d holds an editable install of the package, but `config/` and
    the driver scripts are only in the clone, because Hydra resolves config_path relative to the
    file carrying @hydra.main.

    The copy of `oceanml3d/` under /opt is a BUILD-TIME SNAPSHOT: without PYTHONPATH, an image
    built last week runs last week's package against today's scripts and configs, which surfaces as
    an AttributeError on a function that is plainly there in the clone. jobs/env/_lib.sh
    (container_exec) therefore puts the clone first on PYTHONPATH; do the same if you call
    apptainer directly, and the image then only supplies the dependencies:

        apptainer exec --nv --bind "$PWD":"$PWD" --pwd "$PWD" --env PYTHONPATH="$PWD" \
            oceanml3d.sif oceanml3d experiment=nosc_15m_duacs paths=<site>

    Or, unchanged, to run the package the image was built with:

        apptainer exec --nv --bind "$PWD":"$PWD" --pwd "$PWD" oceanml3d.sif \
            oceanml3d experiment=nosc_15m_duacs paths=<site>

    `--nv` is required: without it the container sees no driver and no GPU, and torch reports
    cuda.is_available() == False with no other complaint.
